}

<https://w3id.org/project-chemie-digital/graph/scenes/level5-illustrated-cases> {
  ex:al-normal-process a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:normal-measurement-model ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-normal ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:al-normal-process-h, ex:al-normal-process-v ; cd:narration "Follow one analytical result through preparation, detector and readout. Each can contribute small random deviations; in an additive error model those contributions add rather than multiply. The three pictured stages stand for many possible influences, not three measured error distributions. Many small non-dominant contributions with suitable finite-variance and dependence conditions can motivate an approximately normal total error. This is not a universal guarantee, and merely adding arbitrary processes does not force a normal distribution. A common bias can move the centre, drift can violate stability, and strong dependence or a dominant heavy-tailed contribution can defeat the approximation. The next slide states the resulting candidate model explicitly using calligraphic capital N, then shows its density."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-normal-process-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "From additive analytical effects to a model"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-normal-process-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-normal-process-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-normal-process-v a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-additive-media ; cd:communicativeRole cd:MediaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:uri" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .

  ex:al-standardization a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:normal-measurement-model ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-z ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:al-standardization-h, ex:al-standardization-v, ex:al-standardization-f ; cd:narration "Apply standardization to a normal measurement model. Subtract the population mean and divide by positive population SD. For example, 10.30 mg/L is one SD above mean 10.00 when SD is 0.30. Uppercase Z is the random standardized quantity; lowercase z is one realized value. Under the normal model, Z follows a standard normal with mean zero and variance one. Standardizing an arbitrary distribution does not make it normal. Density heights rescale, while corresponding event probabilities stay unchanged. This coordinate tool will recur when constructing chi-square, t and F references. The control example uses SD 0.30 mg/L, not the later IC reference SD 0.10 mg/L."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-standardization-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "A reusable tool: distance in standard-deviation units"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-standardization-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-standardization-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-standardization-v a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-standardization-chart ; cd:communicativeRole cd:ChartRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-standardization-f a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 4 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-standardization-formula ; cd:communicativeRole cd:FormulaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:latex" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .

  ex:al-control-story a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:normal-measurement-model ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-normal ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:al-control-story-h, ex:al-control-story-c ; cd:narration "Give the probability calculation an analytical purpose. A laboratory monitors a homogeneous nitrate control material. In this constructed in-control scenario, individual results have specified analytical mean 10.0 mg/L and SD 0.30 mg/L under a normal model. These parameters are supplied for teaching, not inferred from five values. The laboratory considers an illustrative internal review rule: flag an individual result above 10.45 mg/L. This is not a legal or safety threshold. Even an in-control process can exceed the flag threshold because of random analytical variation. We want the model upper flag rate, not a probability that a particular flag proves method failure. After calculating it, we reverse the problem and choose a threshold for a five-percent upper flag rate. This is a one-sided single-result rule, not a full validated control chart or a mean confidence interval."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-control-story-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Usecase: how often does a control result trigger review"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-control-story-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-control-story-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:al-control-story-c a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-control-cards ; cd:communicativeRole cd:DefinitionListRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasDefinitionListEntry" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
}
